      "text": "```\nORAL HISTORY vs. ORAL TRADITION\n================================"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-ORAL-HISTORY.md#003",
      "path": [
        "08-ORAL-HISTORY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:oral-tradition:08-oral-history, git-history:oral-tradition:08-oral-history]",
        "canonical_path": "oral-tradition/08-ORAL-HISTORY.md",
        "concepts": "[oral, history]",
        "current_path": "oral-tradition/08-ORAL-HISTORY.md",
        "id": "maxim:oral-tradition:oral-history",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "oral-tradition",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[oral, history]",
        "section": "oral-tradition",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Oral History as Method: Interview Technique, Archive, Reliability",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "ORAL TRADITION                    ORAL HISTORY\n--------------                    ------------\nMulti-generational transmission   Single-generation recorded memory\nCommunity knowledge               Individual testimony\nNo original recording             Recorded at time of interview\nNo fixed \"interview\"              Structured interview protocol\nEvolves over time                 Fixed at recording\nServes community function         Serves historian / researcher\nStudied by folklorists,           Studied by historians,\nanthropologists                   sociologists, journalists\nExamples: epics, myths,           Examples: Holocaust testimony,\nfolk narrative                    labor history, civil rights\n                                  veterans' accounts"
    },
    {
      "id": "08-ORAL-HISTORY.md#004",
      "path": [
        "08-ORAL-HISTORY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
